From 9ac5ce8db784d34e0e5e6fbe9c77634be3ae8d25 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cpkt9762 <cpkt9762@163.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 04:52:06 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(cloud): add field-level sealing for cloud sync payloads

Cloud sync sends observation content, prompts and session summaries to
the server as plaintext JSON, and the server stores them in a JSONB
column. Anyone who can snapshot that database -- including the hosting
provider, who needs no credentials to do it -- reads the whole corpus.

The obvious fix, encrypting the payload wholesale, does not work here.
The server is not a blob store: InsertMutationBatch unmarshals every
entry to materialize chunks, WriteChunk indexes sessions and counts
entities, and both validate that project / sessions[i].id /
observations[i].sync_id are present. An opaque payload is rejected at
push time.

So only the fields the server never inspects are sealed: title and
content on observations, content on prompts, summary on sessions. The
JSON shape and every structural field survive, so no server change is
needed and materialization keeps working on ciphertext.

AES-256-GCM with a per-seal random nonce, envelope "enc:v1:<base64>".
Random nonces stop the server from spotting that two rows hold identical
content. GCM authenticates, so a wrong key or a tampered payload fails
loudly rather than writing garbage into the local store.

Unsealed values pass through Open untouched. That is what lets a store
holding pre-encryption rows, or a server that still has plaintext, keep
working during rollout.

Key lives at <data-dir>/cloud.key, 0600, mirrored into the macOS login
keychain as a second copy. Losing it makes everything already on the
server unreadable; the local SQLite store stays plaintext, so the blast
radius is the replica -- except on a machine that only ever pulled.

Deliberately NOT hidden: project names, sync ids, session ids,
observation type, scope, topic keys, timestamps and per-project counts.
The server needs those to route, order and deduplicate. An operator
still learns the shape of the work, just not its contents.
